text:
Shares of Apple ( AAPL ) are down two cents at $167.41 in advance of the company’s fiscal Q1 report due this afternoon after the closing bell.
What more need be said, at this point, save for the numbers themselves?
Illustration: Getty Images Apple shares have been tossed about over the past month with a series of cuts estimates for the company’s iPhone sales . The focus in particular is on what may happen this quarter ending in March, more so than the December-ending results.
Analysts are modeling Q1 revenue and profit of $87.5 billion, up almost 12%, and earnings per share of $3.85. Apple’s own forecast was for revenue of $84 billion to $87 billion.
For the March outlook, the Street thinks Apple will forecast something like $65.41 billion and $2.83 per share. That revenue estimate is down from $68.9 billion back in December.
But it still assumes iPhone units of 59 million , which is quite a bit above revised estimates at some shops of 53 million to 55 million.
Included in the iPhone worries is a prediction by some that Apple’s sales in China last quarter were weaker than expected—and may be weaker this quarter as well.
Never mind that at $8.5 billion, Apple’s services business was 16% of revenue in the fiscal Q4 reported in November , up from 13.5% a year earlier. As is so often the case, all the Street cares about in front of an earnings report is that iPhone unit number.
The stock is down 1% so far since the beginning of the year, deeply trailing the Nasdaq’s 7% rise. So much hand-wringing has to have a silver lining, doesn’t it?
